<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/expert/anthropology-of-the-contemporary">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson closes the Anthropology ladder by pointing the discipline's instruments at the
    present. You should arrive from the advanced lessons "Ethnographic methods" — participant
    observation and the emic/etic analysis every study cited here depends on — and "Economic
    and political anthropology", whose gift-and-embeddedness debate Karen Ho's Wall Street
    fieldwork directly extends; "Kinship and marriage systems" (intermediate) supplies the
    classical personhood concepts that Mauss's essay anchors.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the "studying up" programme and name the fieldwork that executed it — Traweek's physicists, Ho's bankers, Seaver's engineers;</li>
    <li>apply socialisation, status and role to contemporary professions and markets;</li>
    <li>analyse personification — corporate persons, chatbots — as a social practice rather than a metaphysical question;</li>
    <li>recognise ritual in organisational life and use the anxiety test to identify it;</li>
    <li>read infrastructure and classification as politics, and globalisation as patches followed person by person.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Anthropology of the contemporary turns the discipline's tools on <strong>the modern world
    itself</strong> — labs, markets, algorithms, bureaucracies, crises. The founding wager: the
    ethnographic gaze that revealed other societies' kinship and cosmology reveals our own
    institutions' logic just as well, including the parts they do not know about themselves.
    This chapter runs the unit's classical concepts — socialisation, status and role, personhood,
    ritual, social movements, modernity, globalisation — through the contemporary fieldwork that
    made each one newly useful. The result is a double lesson: the old concepts still bite, and
    the modern world turns out to be as full of ritual, rank and belief as any field site.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="studying up, inside machines, inside crises" widget="equationStep" config={contempCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps trace the discipline's outward turn in
    order. Step 1 relocates the field site — the laboratory, the bank, the platform are
    cultures with an inside. Step 2 is the licence for that move (Nader's call to study the
    powerful), step 3 its hardest terrain (technology, whose makers often claim culturelessness),
    and step 4 the payoff: crisis response that treats belief as data. Watch how each step
    reuses a classical concept — socialisation, status, ritual — on new ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Individuals, groups and society: the laboratory moved home</h2>
  <p>
    The unit's first topic — how individuals, groups and society constitute each other — became
    the discipline's agenda-setting question when Laura Nader issued the call to "study up"
    (1969/1972): anthropologists had studied the governed long enough; the powerful have cultures
    too, and those cultures have consequences. Sharon Traweek's <em>Beamtimes and Lifetimes</em>
    (1988) answered for particle physics: the international collider communities she followed
    were tribes with a cosmology (the eternal, machine-independent laws of physics), a creation
    myth (the heroic experiment) and a status economy fought out over detector authorship lists.
    Once institutions are read as cultures, everything the discipline knows about symbol,
    hierarchy and belief applies — to boardrooms, regulators and research groups as much as to
    villages.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> once institutions are read as cultures, the discipline's whole
    classical toolkit becomes usable at home. The next sections run its core concepts —
    socialisation first — through the contemporary field sites that made each one newly
    measurable.
  </p>

  <h2>Socialisation: how the professions make their people</h2>
  <p>
    Socialisation — how a person becomes a competent member of a group — is the standard
    contemporary fieldwork observation. Traweek showed physicists are made through decades-long
    apprenticeship: graduate students learn not just theory but what counts as an elegant
    detector, whom to trust, and how to narrate a career. Medical socialisation has its own
    classic findings: students learn hierarchy, emotional detachment ("clinical distance") and
    the informal curriculum that outranks the official one. The technology industry imports
    anthropologists precisely for this — Intel's and Microsoft's practice groups studied how
    engineers and users actually behave, because the official process and the socialised reality
    diverge in ways that determine whether products fail. Socialisation never ends; it just
    changes workplaces.
  </p>

  <h2>Status and role: Wall Street's moral machinery</h2>
  <p>
    Status (rank in a hierarchy) and role (the behavioural package attached to a position)
    organise the contemporary workplace, and Karen Ho's <em>Liquidated</em> (2009) — fieldwork
    on Wall Street after her own stint as an investment banker — is the exemplary study. Ho
    found a culture of "smartness" (prestige recursively defined by employer ranking) and, above
    all, <em>job insecurity as a value</em>: bankers narrated constant layoffs as normal and even
    virtuous, which explains behaviour that economics alone could not — why a firm would gut
    its own experienced workforce every downturn. The role writes the person: analysts internalise
    expendability, and the system gets loyalty through its opposite. It is the strongest single
    demonstration that markets are made of meanings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> socialisation showed professions manufacturing their people, and
    Ho's fieldwork showed status and role producing behaviour that economics alone cannot
    explain. Next comes the sharpest lens on institutional culture — how gender is rationed
    where status is scarce — and then the Maussian question of who counts as a person at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Gender and sexuality: the culture of the algorithm factory</h2>
  <p>
    Contemporary ethnography has documented how gender works inside the industries building
    supposedly neutral systems: the homosocial recruiting cultures (referrals from people like
    us), the status games of 24/7 availability coded masculine, the brogrammer identity that
    makes offices culturally hostile long before any policy does. The corporate answers
    (mentoring, sponsorship, pay audits) are themselves cultural practices whose success varies
    by institution — which is the anthropological point: gender regimes are local, learnable and
    therefore changeable. Ho's Wall Street had its own gendered economics (women concentrated in
    support roles, "front office" masculinity priced in bonuses); the pattern generalises —
    where status is scarce, gender is how it is rationed.
  </p>

  <h2>Personhood: from Mauss to corporate persons and chatbots</h2>
  <p>
    Marcel Mauss's classic essay defined personhood as a category with a history — a legal and
    moral construction that different societies fill differently (the ancestor-containing Roman
    person, the relational Melanesian person made of exchanges). Contemporary anthropology
    extends the list: the corporation is a legal person (Citizens United, 2010, extended its
    speech rights in the US); algorithms are acquiring quasi-personhood by social acclamation —
    people apologise to chatbots, credit them with intent, and grieve their shutdowns. The
    analytical move is not to adjudicate whether a model "really" has a mind but to study
    <em>personification as a social practice</em>: what work does treating a system as someone
    do — for its designers, its users, its regulators? The Maussian answer is already in place:
    personhood expands to fit the institutions that need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "anthropology still means studying remote tribes." That was
    the discipline's founding setting (Malinowski's Trobriands, 1914–18), but since the 1960s
    its field sites include hospitals, trading floors, laboratories, data centres and refugee
    camps. The unit of analysis is always the same — people making worlds together — not a
    location on a map.
  </p>

  <h2>Social and group identity: engineers, bankers and the tribe next door</h2>
  <p>
    Group identity in modern institutions is forged the way it is anywhere: against outsiders
    and through shared ordeal. Nick Seaver's fieldwork among music-recommendation engineers
    found occupational identities built by distinction — developers defined themselves against
    "marketers" and "users", and their taste judgments about what music "is" quietly shaped
    what the algorithm could recommend. Investment bankers formed an identity around
    <em>shared</em> expendability (Ho); physicists around membership in a species of
    exceptionalists (Traweek). Identity is also the fieldwork's data problem: the ethnographer
    is socialised too, and modern anthropology's reflexive turn — writing the observer into the
    account — began exactly where the observer most resembles the observed.
  </p>

  <h2>Ritual: standups, launches and the liturgy of organisations</h2>
  <p>
    Ritual — formalised, repeated, symbolically loaded collective action — survived modernity
    and moved indoors. The daily standup, the all-hands, the hackathon, the product launch with
    its keynote liturgy, the IPO bell-ringing: these are rites of aggregation, intensification
    and passage in Victorian anthropologists' exact sense. Harrison White's network sociology
    and the ethnomethodologists showed how meeting ritual produces organisational reality
    (what is decided in the corridor counts only after the meeting ratifies it). The useful
    test: a practice is functioning as ritual when skipping it creates anxiety out of
    proportion to its information content — which describes the standup precisely, and explains
    why remote work broke something rituals used to carry.
  </p>

  <h2>Social movements: Occupy as an anthropological object</h2>
  <p>
    Social movements are where contemporary anthropology meets public life, sometimes through
    its own members: David Graeber — anarchist, author of <em>Debt: The First 5000 Years</em>
    (2011) — was a formative organiser in Occupy Wall Street (2011), which made consensus
    process and general assemblies themselves an ethnographic spectacle: a prefigurative
    movement building the society it demanded, with hand signals replacing chairs. The
    anthropological contributions are distinctive: movements as culture (values, rituals,
    identity work), not just resource mobilisation; and the study of how grievances become
    movements through media and ritual. The same toolkit now reads climate activism,
    #MeToo and the far right — the method does not editorialise about which movements deserve
    study.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "modernity means the end of ritual — rational societies
  outgrow ceremony." Watch any product launch, graduation, courtroom session or daily
  stand-up: rituality was not abandoned, it was re-staffed. Rites of transition, solidarity
  and institutional time-keeping are as load-bearing in a tech company as in a village —
  just less willing to name themselves so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> identity, ritual and movements have all turned out to be alive and
    well inside modern institutions. The remaining sections take the two largest concepts —
    modernity and globalisation — and show that their defining artefacts are precisely the ones
    we are trained not to look at.
  </p>

  <h2>Modernity: infrastructure and its invisibility</h2>
  <p>
    Modernity's defining artefact, Susan Leigh Star argued in "The Ethnography of
    Infrastructure" (1999), is invisible: roads, standards, cables and classifications
    disappear into the background until they break. Studying modernity therefore means
    studying plumbing — how standards bodies negotiate a plug shape, how classifications
    (ICD codes, racial categories in databases) silently govern what becomes countable and
    therefore governable. Geoffrey Bowker and Star's <em>Sorting Things Out</em> (1999) made
    classification the discipline's gateway concept for modern institutions: categories are
    infrastructure, and infrastructure is politics by other means. The anthropological
    payoff of modernity studies is a habit of mind: whenever something seems too boring to
    examine, look there.
  </p>

  <h2>Globalisation: the matsutake supply chain</h2>
  <p>
    Globalisation studies found its exemplary ethnography in Anna Tsing's <em>The Mushroom at
    the End of the World</em> (2015): following the matsutake mushroom from Oregon regrowth
    forests through pickers, buyers, graders and flights to Japan's gift markets — a global
    commodity chain assembled from refugees, shifting cultivators and opportunistic commerce,
    with no central coordination at all. Arjun Appadurai's theory (-scapes: mediascapes,
    ethnoscapes, financescapes moving at different speeds) supplies the frame; Tsing supplies
    the fieldwork that shows globalisation is <em>patches</em> — contingent collaborations, not
    a system with a controller. Contemporary crises are read the same way: supply chains,
    migration corridors and platform economies are followed person by person, because the
    aggregate view is exactly what hides the people.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "infrastructure is neutral plumbing — pipes, rails and
  cables without politics." Every standard embodies a choice: gauge widths, plug voltages,
  terms of service and protocol defaults quietly decide who connects easily and who needs
  adapters. Infrastructure is where power hides best — which is why it becomes visible
  mainly in failure, and why this field spends so much time with maintenance crews.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What did Laura Nader's "study up" call ask for, and which study answered it for particle physics?</li>
    <li>Using the concepts of status and role, explain how Ho's bankers came to narrate constant layoffs as normal and even virtuous.</li>
    <li>Why does the test "anxiety out of proportion to information content" catch the daily standup as ritual — and what does that finding do to the claim that modern societies outgrow ceremony?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Nader (1969/1972) called for anthropologists to study the
    powerful, not only the governed — the powerful have cultures too, with consequences. Sharon
    Traweek's <em>Beamtimes and Lifetimes</em> (1988) answered for particle physics, reading
    collider communities as tribes with a cosmology, a creation myth and a status economy.
    (2) Status is rank in a hierarchy; role is the behavioural package attached to a position.
    In the culture of "smartness" Ho found, the analyst role packages expendability as virtue —
    internalising it is what competence in the role means — so the system gets loyalty through
    its opposite. The role writes the person. (3) Skipping a standup produces anxiety far beyond
    the meeting's information content, which is the signature of ritual: aggregation, time-keeping
    and ratification of what the corridor decided. Modernity therefore re-staffed ritual rather
    than abandoning it — the rites moved indoors and stopped naming themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The ethnographic gaze works at home: labs, markets, algorithms and bureaucracies are cultures with an inside.</li>
    <li>"Studying up" (Nader) made boardrooms, regulators and standards bodies legitimate field sites.</li>
    <li>Socialisation never ends, it changes workplaces — professions manufacture their people (Traweek's physicists, clinical distance).</li>
    <li>Ho's Wall Street shows status and role producing behaviour economics alone cannot explain: markets are made of meanings.</li>
    <li>Personhood is a category with a history (Mauss); corporations are legal persons and chatbots acquire quasi-personhood by acclamation.</li>
    <li>Ritual survived modernity indoors — standups, launches and IPO bells are rites in the classic sense.</li>
    <li>Classification is infrastructure and infrastructure is politics (Bowker and Star); Tsing shows globalisation as patches, not a system with a controller.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson "Deep history of our species" swings the same lens from the
    present back across the species' whole past, weighing five rival kinds of history — agency,
    cohesion, conflict, idealist, materialist — against the archaeological record. For the
    method behind every study cited here, return to the advanced lesson "Ethnographic methods":
    long residence, emic and etic analysis, and the reflexive writing of the observer into the
    account.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Anthropology of crisis: belief systems, not ignorance.</strong> The applied face
    of the subfield is epidemic response. Vaccine hesitancy, studied ethnographically, turns
    out to be neither ignorance nor refusal-as-quirk but situated reasoning: histories of
    medical exploitation (Tuskegee's legacy in Black American communities; Pakistani polio
    vaccine politics entangled with the fake hepatitis campaign used to locate bin Laden in
    2011), institutional distrust, and locally coherent risk calculations. The finding is
    operational: supply-side campaigns that treat hesitancy as a belief system — working
    through trusted local figures, addressing the specific history — outperform campaigns
    that treat it as an information deficit. Pandemic anthropology, migration ethnography
    and disaster studies now run on this logic: how communities experience the crisis
    determines what the crisis does, which is knowledge no epidemiological model contains.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Is ethnography "just anecdotes"?</strong> The methodological answer defines the
    subfield's standards: long residence (months to years), triangulation across
    observation, interview and participation, systematic comparison within the site, and
    explicit theoretical claims about mechanisms — the same standards as any qualitative
    science, with the ethnographer's own positionality documented rather than denied. What
    ethnography trades away in sample size it gains in mechanism access: surveys reveal
    <em>that</em> bankers are insecure; Ho's fieldwork reveals <em>how</em> insecurity is
    produced, valued and reproduced — the difference between a finding and its machinery.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Method: multi-sited ethnography.</strong> George Marcus's 1995 formulation
    legitimated following the thing — the commodity, the metaphor, the technology — across sites
    rather than dwelling in one place, which is what made globalisation, supply chains and
    platform capitalism tractable. The cost is the classic trade-off: breadth of connection
    against depth of residence, and the field still argues about how to reconcile them
    (Tsing-style chain-following with local anchoring is one influential answer).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The industry marriage.</strong> Lucy Suchman's <em>Plans and Situated Actions</em>
    (1987), conducted inside Xerox PARC, founded user-centred design: photocopiers failed
    because engineers designed for idealised plans, not situated human behaviour — and a decade
    of corporations hiring anthropologists followed (Intel, Microsoft, ReD Associates). The
    current frontier is algorithmic systems: ethnographers embedded in ML teams documenting how
    training data gets made and values get encoded — the discipline's critique functioning, for
    once, from inside the machine room.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can the discipline study AI systems at scale without
    abandoning its method's core (long residence, small numbers)? How does fieldwork handle
    populations of billions (the platform problem)? And after decades of "studying up", has the
    balance shifted — or do funders, access and risk still tilt the field toward the governed?
    The debates are live in the discipline's own journals, which is the healthy sign.
  </p>

